Debt Strategy

  The Math Behind It

  Every dollar of high-interest debt you pay off is a guaranteed, risk-free return equal to the interest rate. Paying off a 20% APR credit
  card is a guaranteed 20% return — better than any investment.

  The break-even line is roughly 6–7%. Below that, investing often wins. Above it, pay the debt first.

  Credit card (22%)    → Pay off immediately, always
  Personal loan (12%)  → Pay off before investing beyond 401k match
  Student loan (6%)    → Coin flip — both reasonable, lean toward investing
  Mortgage (4%)        → Invest instead; also tax-deductible

  ---
  Avalanche Method (Saves the Most Money)
  
  Pay minimums on everything. Throw every extra dollar at the highest interest rate debt first.

  Debt A: $5,000 @ 22% APR  ← Attack this first
  Debt B: $8,000 @ 10% APR
  Debt C: $2,000 @  5% APR

  Example savings: if you have $500/month extra, avalanche vs. snowball can save you thousands and months of payments on a typical debt load.

  ---
  Snowball Method (Psychological Wins)

  Pay minimums on everything. Attack the smallest balance first regardless of rate.

  Debt C: $2,000 @  5% APR  ← Attack this first
  Debt A: $5,000 @ 22% APR
  Debt B: $8,000 @ 10% APR

  You get quick wins that keep you motivated. Research shows many people succeed more with snowball because they don't quit. The best method 
  is the one you'll stick to.

  ---
  Debt Payoff Accelerators
  
  - Balance transfer cards — 0% APR for 12–21 months (usually 3–5% transfer fee). Aggressively pay it down during the promo window.
  - Refinancing — if your credit score improved, you may qualify for a lower rate on student loans or personal loans
  - Avoid minimum payments — a $5,000 card at 22% APR on minimums takes 17+ years and costs $7,000+ in interest
  - Debt consolidation loans — can work, but only if the rate is actually lower and you don't run the cards back up

