  Investing in the Stock Market

  How Stocks Actually Work

  When you buy a share, you own a small piece of a real company — its earnings, assets, and future growth. Stocks rise over time because
  companies earn profits, reinvest them, and grow. The S&P 500 has never had a negative 20-year return in history.

  ---
  The Two Ways to Make Money
  
  1. Price appreciation — you buy at $100, sell at $150
  2. Dividends — companies pay you a share of profits quarterly (e.g., 1.5%/year)

  Total return = price appreciation + dividends reinvested.

  ---
  Individual Stocks vs. Index Funds

  Individual stocks:
  - Requires deep research per company
  - One bad earnings call can drop 30% overnight
  - ~80% of professional fund managers fail to beat the index over 10 years
  - Fine for a small "fun money" slice (5–10% of portfolio max)

  Index funds:
  - Own hundreds or thousands of companies in one ticker
  - Instant diversification — one company going bankrupt barely moves the needle
  - Lower fees (0.03% vs 0.5–1%+ for active funds)
  - Historically outperform most professionals over the long run

  ---
  The Power of Compounding (Why Starting Early Destroys Everything Else)
  
  Assuming 7% average annual return (inflation-adjusted S&P 500 historical average):

  ┌───────────┬────────────────────┬────────────┐
  │ Start Age │ Monthly Investment │ At Age 65  │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ 22        │ $300               │ $1,000,000 │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ 32        │ $300               │ $491,000   │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ 42        │ $300               │ $227,000   │
  ├───────────┼────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ 22        │ $150               │ $500,000   │
  └───────────┴────────────────────┴────────────┘

  Starting 10 years earlier with the same amount roughly doubles your outcome. Amount invested matters less than time invested.

