  Market Crashes Are Normal — and Necessary
  
  ┌───────────────────────┬──────┬───────────┐
  │         Crash         │ Drop │ Recovery  │
  ├───────────────────────┼──────┼───────────┤
  │ 2000 Dot-com          │ -49% │ ~7 years  │
  ├───────────────────────┼──────┼───────────┤
  │ 2008 Financial Crisis │ -57% │ ~5 years  │
  ├───────────────────────┼──────┼───────────┤
  │ 2020 COVID            │ -34% │ ~6 months │
  ├───────────────────────┼──────┼───────────┤
  │ 2022 Bear market      │ -25% │ ~1 year   │
  └───────────────────────┴──────┴───────────┘

  The market has always recovered and gone on to new highs. The only people who locked in losses were those who sold during the drop.

  Crashes are when long-term investors get rich — your fixed monthly contribution buys far more shares at depressed prices.

  ---
  Reading a Stock or Fund

  VTI — Vanguard Total Stock Market ETF

  Price:          $240.00
  Expense ratio:  0.03%       ← annual fee you pay (very low)
  Dividend yield: 1.3%        ← annual cash paid per share
  52-week range:  $195–$255   ← ignore this for long-term investing
  AUM:            $450B        ← how much money is in it (bigger = stable)

  For index funds, the only number that should drive your decision is the expense ratio. Everything else is noise if you're investing for 20+
   years.

  ---
  Taxes on Investments (Taxable Accounts)

  ┌────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Holding period │                 Tax rate                  │
  ├────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Sold < 1 year  │ Ordinary income rate (22–37%)             │
  ├────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Sold > 1 year  │ Long-term capital gains (0%, 15%, or 20%) │
  └────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Key takeaway: hold investments for at least 1 year before selling. For most people, long-term capital gains are taxed at 15% vs. 22–24% for
   short-term.

  In retirement accounts (Roth IRA, 401k) — none of this applies. Growth is either tax-deferred or tax-free.

  ---
  Rebalancing
  
  Over time, your portfolio drifts. If stocks do well, you might go from 80/20 stocks-bonds to 90/10. Rebalancing means selling some stocks
  and buying bonds to get back to target.

  - Do it once a year (not more — it triggers taxes in taxable accounts)
  - Or use new contributions to buy the underweight asset instead of selling

  ---
  The Simplest Possible Strategy That Wins
  
  1. Open a Roth IRA at Fidelity or Vanguard (free, takes 10 minutes)
  2. Set up automatic monthly transfers
  3. Buy VTI (total US market) or FSKAX (Fidelity equivalent)
  4. Never look at it during a crash
  5. Repeat for 30 years
